For working during the course you will need to install the needed software on your computer. In this document you will find the installation recommendations and steps you must follow to make your computer ready for the course. 

# Prior recommendations
When working with R and other languages and software there are some recommendations that would help us avoid errors and future problems running our code, here are some useful tips we strongly recommend to follow:

1. **IMPORTANT** Do not use special characters, blank spaces when naming files, folders. This will avoid problems when installing and executing code in the future. 

2. It is strongly recommended to show file extensions on all the files. A file extension is the text you can find below the file name, for example in ```synopsis.txt```, the ```.txt``` part tell us is a text file. Generally, this extensions are visible in majority of operative systems, for Windows users it is hidden by default, but you can change it using [**this tutorial**](https://www.howtogeek.com/205086/beginner-how-to-make-windows-show-file-extensions/).

# Installing R and RStudio
We are going to learn R, so you will need to install it in your computer. This will let you use it and your computer understand it for executing the code. 

R is maintained by an international team of developers who make the language available through the web page of [**The Comprehensive R Archive Network**](https://cran.r-project.org/). The top of the web page provides three links for downloading R. Follow the link that describes your operating system (OS): Windows, Mac, or Linux.

For using R you would need to have a development environment (IDE), a set of tools for working properly and take the whole potential of the programming language. RStudio will be the IDE we are going to use...

  * It is the same for every OS.
  * It is widely use by everyone who works with R
  * It is free!

You can download RStudio for desktop, from its [**official website**](https://www.rstudio.com/products/rstudio/), and there is also an online version in case you needed.

For installing all, you can follow [**this chapter**](https://rstudio-education.github.io/hopr/starting.html#how-to-download-and-install-r) from an online book that explains with detail how to install it in each operative system. You can find also tutorial throughout the internet to help you (videos, blogs, etc.).
